# broiler-shared-package pin 1
#
# One package of the committed, hash-pinned feed (decision SJL-D-4 (c); milestone SJL-2.7): a shared
# JavaScript library package whose home is the Broiler.JS repository, retained here byte for byte so
# that a restore needs no other repository's folder and no network for it. NuGet.config's package
# source mapping sends the Broiler.ECMAScript prefix to this directory and to nuget.org; the lock
# file of a project that references it records `content-hash`, and rule I1 holds that hash to this
# pin and to the allow-list.
#
# HOW IT WAS TAKEN. The package exactly as the Broiler.JS repository's publish-shared workflow pushed
# it to nuget.org on 2026-10-15 (run 38693270741, its artifact shared-packages), packed from
# `upstream-commit`; the nuspec's repository element records the same commit, which rule I7 reads.
# nuget.org serves the same package with its repository signature (.signature.p7s) added, and NuGet's
# content hash of a signed package leaves the signature out, so a restore from either source records
# `content-hash`, the base64 SHA-512 of this file's bytes. `sha256` is over the same bytes.
#
# (Revised 2026-10-15, when the package was published. Until then this feed held a copy packed locally
# from Broiler.JS 0db9042. The published commit changes nothing under Broiler.ECMAScript/ outside its
# tests, so the library code is the same; the package differs in the commit its nuspec and assembly
# record, the case of the repository URL, and its THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md, whose sentence on Broiler.JS's
# UriHelper SJL-5.5 revised. The paragraph read: "Packed on 2026-10-15 in the Broiler.JS repository,
# from `upstream-commit`, with `dotnet pack Broiler.ECMAScript/Broiler.ECMAScript.Algorithms -c Release
# -p:ContinuousIntegrationBuild=true`; Broiler.ECMAScript/Directory.Build.props sets the version,
# 0.1.0-preview.3, at that commit. The nuspec's repository element records the same commit, which rule
# I7 reads. `content-hash` is NuGet's: the base64 SHA-512 of the .nupkg's bytes, which is what a lock
# file records. `sha256` is over the same bytes. It is not on nuget.org: a Broiler.VM package release
# that depends on it waits for its publication (eng/verify-feed.ps1), and the pin is then taken again
# from the published package, as 0.1.0-preview.2's was." The identity lines read:
#   content-hash PGmwRgBhD2LYe7KgV8xUTh4Lm8Os11BAxJhYcLdmultCvLFltDaNgZlPKgFvQr/nACAmw/v8B0Rpn7jqZ53Sgw==
#   sha256 8185eb68d56a37ee7ad0ff49ec66f999c3784b93d299c1651664afc47ddd4567
#   upstream-commit 0db90422c8aca642926acff6b715d788cb17f9c6)
#
# WHAT IT IS. The text area of the shared algorithms library (milestone SJL-5.3; JSD-0070), namespace
# Broiler.ECMAScript.Text, with the number-conversion and numerics areas of 0.1.0-preview.2 unchanged:
# admitted for PRODUCT use by Broiler.VM.Profile.JavaScript and Broiler.VM.Profile.JavaScript.Compiler
# at SJL-5.4, and restored by the admission probe. It carries no third-party component beyond
# 0.1.0-preview.2's, which its THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md names. It is on nuget.org since 2026-10-15, as a
# Broiler.VM package release that depends on it requires (eng/verify-feed.ps1).

id Broiler.ECMAScript.Algorithms
version 0.1.0-preview.3
file Broiler.ECMAScript.Algorithms.0.1.0-preview.3.nupkg
content-hash cLMz53DAFAhaA1pJPk6migLRBygv7tyjhXw4gSd2GI3GQ6b7eCRFEEFAQ4wHNnHZvy2ppVsIIF1pG+BEUl2zTg==
sha256 381e35ea6533de8ba689df7f88ad1600a4e36924524c2d2be13f0e6a7625233d
upstream Broiler-Platform/broiler.js
upstream-commit 6e20998fae9b4124ab3051a773c5348eca6feca3
